  "intro_title_en": "How to read this book",
  "whats_next": {
    "title": "Что да́льше",
    "lang": "ru",
    "body": [
      "Тепе́рь чита́й «Пикни́к на обо́чине» Стругацких: пять страни́ц в день.",
      "Выпи́сывай но́вые слова́ и чита́й да́льше на сле́дующий день."
    ],
    "note": "The list of the novel's 300 most frequent lemmas not taught in the course is added only if it can be computed from the owner's copy without redistributing the text (design §13)."
  },
  "parts": [
    {
      "number": 1,
      "part": 1,
      "title": "Дере́вня",
      "from": 1,
      "to": 10,
      "dates": "spring–December 1913",
      "afterword_title_en": "The village in 1913",
      "afterword_en": "In 1913 four Russians in five lived in villages. Most peasant land in central Russia belonged not to households but to the commune (the mir, or obshchina): the village assembly of household heads (the skhod) shared the fields out in narrow strips, set the farming calendar of the three-field rotation, apportioned taxes and could redistribute the strips. Collective liability for taxes had been abolished by 1907, but the commune still decided almost everything.\n\nFrom 1906 the prime minister Pyotr Stolypin's reforms let a household take its strips out of the commune as private property and consolidate them into one plot (an otrub; a khutor if the farmhouse moved onto it). Between 1907 and 1915 nearly half of all households applied for land settlement, but only a minority, fewer in the central provinces, actually separated. Fyodor's request in Chapter 9 is typical of 1913, and so is the assembly's refusal.\n\nLiteracy was spreading through zemstvo schools: about a fifth of the Empire could read in 1897, and by 1913 roughly three army recruits in four were at least semi-literate. Grandfather Matvei was born a serf; the emancipation of 1861 freed the serfs but left them paying for land many thought was theirs already.",
      "afterword_sources": [
        "https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Сельский_сход",
        "https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Столыпинская_аграрная_реформа",
        "https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Грамотность_в_России"
      ]
    },
    {
      "number": 2,
      "part": 2,
      "title": "Война́",
      "from": 11,
      "to": 20,
      "dates": "July 1914 – spring 1916",
      "afterword_title_en": "The war, 1914–1916",
      "afterword_en": "Russia mobilised on 17 (30) July 1914; Germany declared war on 19 July (1 August). The first call-up took trained reservists such as Fyodor; younger men like Ivan, born in 1894, were called early, in January 1915, trained for a few weeks in a reserve battalion and sent to the front in march companies. The Ryazan regiments of the 35th Infantry Division served on the Southwestern Front against Austria-Hungary.\n\nFrom May 1915 the German and Austro-Hungarian breakthrough at Gorlice–Tarnów drove the Russian armies out of Galicia and Poland in the Great Retreat. Wounded men travelled for weeks through dressing stations, field hospitals, evacuation points and hospital trains to rear cities; Ryazan had its own evacuation point and zemstvo infirmaries.\n\nIn the villages the sale of vodka was banned, prices rose, and women ran the farms and learnt to read soldiers' letters.",
      "afterword_sources": [
        "https://conf.msu.ru/archive/Lomonosov_2016/data/8375/uid104786_report.pdf",
        "https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/35-я_пехотная_дивизия_(Российская_империя)",
        "https://militera.lib.ru/research/golovnin_nn/04.html"
      ]
    },
    {
      "number": 3,
      "part": 3,
      "title": "Пра́вда",
      "from": 21,
      "to": 30,
      "dates": "May 1916 – December 1916",
      "afterword_title_en": "Truth and rumour, 1916",
      "afterword_en": "In June 1916 General Brusilov's offensive broke the Austro-Hungarian lines in Volhynia and Galicia: the Russian army's greatest success of the war, bought with enormous losses. Behind the front, food and fuel ran short in the cities, prices climbed, and rumours of treason at court (Rasputin, the German-born Empress) spread through trenches and villages alike.\n\nSocialist ideas reached the front with men like Semyon, a Petrograd metalworker sent to the army as punishment for striking. In the winter of 1916–17 strikes and bread queues in Petrograd prepared the revolution of February 1917.",
      "afterword_sources": [
        "https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Брусиловский_прорыв"
      ]
    },
    {
      "number": 4,
      "part": 4,
      "title": "Во́ля",
      "from": 31,
      "to": 40,
      "dates": "March 1917 – August 1918; coda 1962",
      "afterword_title_en": "Freedom and land, 1917–1918",
      "afterword_en": "In February 1917 the monarchy fell. On 1 March the Petrograd Soviet's Order No. 1 told soldiers to elect committees and forbade officers to address them as ty. At Easter soldiers fraternised with Austrian troops across the lines; the June offensive collapsed as units refused to advance. On 25–26 October the Bolsheviks took power and issued the Decrees on Peace and on Land; peasants divided landlords' estates themselves, often equally by the number of mouths in each household. In the Constituent Assembly elections of November 1917 most villages voted for the Socialist Revolutionaries; the Assembly was dispersed in January 1918.\n\nThe orthographic reform removed ѣ, і, ѳ and final ъ (decreed December 1917, in force October 1918). In May 1918 the Soviet government declared a food dictatorship; armed requisitioning detachments reached Ryazan province that spring and summer.",
      "afterword_sources": [
        "https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Приказ_№_1",
        "https://ru.wikisource.org/wiki/Декрет_о_мире",
        "https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Декрет_о_земле",
        "https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Реформа_русской_орфографии_1918_года"
      ]
    }
  ],
