  "intro_en": "This book teaches Russian the way Lingua Latina per se Illustrata teaches Latin: one continuous story, written entirely in Russian, read in order. It follows Ivan Sokolov, a peasant from a village in Ryazan province, from the spring of 1913 through the war and the revolutions to the summer of 1918.\n\nYou already know some Russian. The first chapters will feel easy: they install the case system on words you half-know. Difficulty rises from Chapter 11 (aspect) and again from Chapter 21.\n\nEvery word carries a stress mark (а́). Stress is shown by default in Parts 1–2 and hidden from Part 3; the toggle works at any time, and cards, glosses and grammar tables always show it. The letter ё is always written with its dots and is always stressed.\n\nThe margin is in Russian only. \"=\" gives a simpler way to say the same thing; \"↔\" an opposite; \"шёл ← идти\" points from an unfamiliar form to its dictionary form; \"сказать pf. ← говорить\" pairs aspects; small tables show three forms of a new pattern. A small picture names an object.\n\nEach chapter has a stepper. Do the steps in order and you never have to decide what to do next: read a section (with audio), do its exercises, repeat for each section; read the Grammar and the Word with a History once; do Exercises A, B and C; write the short text and correct it against the checklist; re-read the whole chapter with stress marks off; download the chapter's Anki cards; press Done. Any step can be skipped, and nothing is locked behind a score.\n\nThe Anki export skips words your existing Russian deck already has as mature cards (you can include them). Cards go to the deck Русский::Мир.\n\nThe Russian is modern standard Russian with period vocabulary. The chapters are drafts written with AI assistance and have not yet been checked by a native speaker; corrections are welcome."
}
